---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const gh = (n: number) => `https://github.com/s-andrews/FastQC/pull/${n}`;
const issue = (n: number) => `https://github.com/s-andrews/FastQC/issues/${n}`;
---

<Base title="Upstream contributions - FastQC" description="Improvements to Java FastQC that came out of the Rust rewrite">
  <p class="label">Upstream contributions</p>
  <h1>Changes to FastQC</h1>

  <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    The Rust rewrite works as a development sandbox for FastQC. Ideas get prototyped and benchmarked here,
    and the equivalence tests catch bugs. What works then goes back to
    <a href="https://github.com/s-andrews/FastQC">Java FastQC</a> as pull requests.
    The aim is a better canonical tool, not a replacement for it.
    Many of these changes shipped in
    <a href="https://github.com/s-andrews/FastQC/releases/tag/v0.13.0">FastQC v0.13.0</a>.
  </p>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>Released in FastQC v0.13.0</h2>

    <h3>Performance</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li>
        <a href={gh(197)}>#197</a> — <strong>About 3x faster with parallel processing.</strong>
        Each file now runs through a small pipeline: one reader thread (decompression and parsing)
        feeds up to three processor threads, each of which owns a subset of the QC modules.
        <code>-t</code> is now a total CPU budget. This work started with
        <a href="https://github.com/pditommaso">Paolo Di Tommaso</a>'s
        <a href="https://github.com/pditommaso/FastQC/pulls">experiments</a>. Each change was then
        benchmarked on its own to find the smallest change with the biggest effect.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={gh(199)}>#199</a> — Removes the per-sequence <code>char[]</code> allocations in seven QC modules.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>SVG graphs</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li>
        <a href={gh(200)}>#200</a> — <strong>SVG is now the default graph format</strong> in the HTML report.
        Graphs are sharp at any zoom level and easy to edit for publication. Use <code>--png</code> to get PNG graphs again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={gh(188)}>#188</a> — Smaller SVG output. Uses presentation attributes, merges line segments into
        polylines and merges runs of same-colour rectangles. Data lines are also thicker.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Correctness</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li>
        <a href={gh(187)}>#187</a> — Adapter Content showed empty trailing rows when <code>--min_length</code> was set.
        The Rust equivalence tests found this. Upstream then reworked <code>--min_length</code> into a real read-length filter,
        and added <code>--max_length</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={issue(209)}>#209</a> — Encoding detection misread high-quality data (no base below Q31) as Illumina 1.5,
        so every quality score was 31 too low. The Rust rewrite found this with BAM input.
        In v0.13.0 Phred+33 is the default for all input, and <code>--phred64</code> is now an opt-in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={issue(203)}>#203</a> — Basic Statistics now reports <strong>mean and median read length</strong>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Project infrastructure</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li><a href={gh(156)}>#156</a> — GitHub Actions workflow that builds the release assets automatically.</li>
      <li><a href={gh(155)}>#155</a> — Markdown README and installation docs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>Merged into a feature branch</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 0.75rem">
      These are merged into the upstream <code>liberationfont</code> branch, but that branch is not yet in <code>master</code>
      (<a href={issue(216)}>#216</a>). <a href={gh(217)}>#217</a> merges it into <code>master</code>.
    </p>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li>
        <a href={gh(185)}>#185</a>, <a href={gh(186)}>#186</a>, <a href={gh(194)}>#194</a> — Bundle the
        Liberation Sans font and anti-alias graph text. This removes the dependency on system fonts,
        so graphs look the same on all machines. It also aligns text metrics between Java and Rust.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>Open pull requests</h2>
    <ul style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      <li><a href={gh(205)}>#205</a> — Mean read length to one decimal place, and the standard median for an even number of reads.</li>
      <li><a href={gh(196)}>#196</a>, <a href={gh(195)}>#195</a> — Move the HTML report layout into template fragments, and fix some small HTML problems.</li>
      <li>
        <a href={gh(161)}>#161</a> — HTML report design refresh. The Rust rewrite already has this as the
        <a href="../../templates/">modern template</a> (<code>--template modern</code>).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      The Rust rewrite follows upstream releases (see <a href="../strategy/">strategy</a>).
      The <a href="../equivalence/">equivalence tests</a> run the Java and Rust versions on the same inputs
      and compare their outputs. A difference is either a Rust bug or a Java bug.
      Java bugs become upstream issues and pull requests.
      When upstream releases a fix, the Rust rewrite gets the same change, so the two outputs stay identical.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
